// Practice quiz: GROUP BY and HAVING
// Formative, low-stakes retention practice. Titles name the objective id each item practises.
// GIFT forbids the angle-bracket characters and treats colon, equals, hash, tilde and braces as syntax, so those are escaped.

$CATEGORY: sql-jdbc-foundations/crud-aggregates-and-joins/group-by-and-having

::o1 rows per group::A staff table has 7 rows spread over 3 departments. How many rows does SELECT department, COUNT(*) FROM staff GROUP BY department; return? {
=3#Correct. GROUP BY returns one result row per distinct grouping value.
~7#Not quite. That is the number of input rows, before grouping.
~1#Not quite. One row is what an aggregate returns without GROUP BY.
~10#Not quite. Groups are not added to the input rows.
}

::o1 NULL group::Two staff rows have department NULL. How does GROUP BY department treat them? {
=They form one group of their own#Correct. For grouping, NULL values are placed together in a single group.
~Each NULL row becomes its own group#Not quite. All NULL values share one group.
~They are dropped from the result#Not quite. Grouping keeps them; only a WHERE filter would remove them.
~They join the first department#Not quite. NULL is never merged into another group.
}

::o2 where the count test goes::A report keeps only departments with at least 3 staff. Where does the condition on COUNT(*) belong? {
=In HAVING, after GROUP BY#Correct. HAVING filters groups after the aggregates are computed.
~In WHERE, before GROUP BY#Not quite. WHERE runs before groups exist, so it cannot test COUNT(*).
~In ORDER BY#Not quite. ORDER BY sorts results; it does not remove groups.
~Inside the SELECT list#Not quite. Selecting a count displays it but filters nothing.
}

::o2 why not WHERE::Why does WHERE COUNT(*) BETWEEN 3 AND 10 fail? {
=WHERE filters single input rows before any groups or counts exist#Correct. Aggregates are computed after WHERE, so WHERE cannot refer to them.
~BETWEEN cannot be used with numbers#Not quite. BETWEEN works with numbers; the problem is the aggregate.
~COUNT(*) needs a column name#Not quite. COUNT(*) is valid, just not inside WHERE.
~WHERE must come after GROUP BY#Not quite. WHERE comes before GROUP BY, which is exactly why it cannot see the groups.
}

::o3 fix the ungrouped column::SELECT department, name, COUNT(*) FROM staff GROUP BY department; fails because name is neither grouped nor aggregated. Which change fixes it and keeps one row per department? {
=Remove name, or summarize it with an aggregate such as MIN(name)#Correct. Each department row needs one value per column, so an ungrouped column must be removed or aggregated.
~Add DISTINCT after SELECT#Not quite. DISTINCT does not decide which name represents a group.
~Add name to ORDER BY#Not quite. Sorting by name does not group or aggregate it.
~Add WHERE name IS NOT NULL#Not quite. Filtering rows still leaves several names per department.
}

::o3 which query errors::orders(order_id, customer_id, total) has primary key order_id. Which query raises an ERROR? {
=SELECT customer_id, total FROM orders GROUP BY customer_id;#Correct. total is neither grouped nor aggregated, and one customer can have several totals, so there is no single value to show.
~SELECT customer_id, SUM(total) FROM orders GROUP BY customer_id;#Not quite. SUM summarizes the totals for each customer, so the query runs.
~SELECT customer_id, COUNT(*) FROM orders GROUP BY customer_id;#Not quite. The grouping key plus an aggregate is always valid.
~SELECT order_id, total FROM orders GROUP BY order_id;#Not quite. order_id is the primary key, so it determines total; PostgreSQL accepts this.
}
